w leader who's come on the scene  and says exactly what a lot of people  are thinking and he's gathered this  agglomeration of voters and supporters  who have many different issues from  ecologist to people concerned about the  Internet to people concerned about the  direct democracy to people who want to  get out of the eurozone people who are  concerned about unemployment a whole  range of issues let me ask you this how  involved are the Italians in the  election what is how many come out to  vote percentage-wise well in this  election about 75 percent voted now that  looks really good from our point of view  that looks great but from the Italian  point of view that's the lowest turnout  they've had since 1946 so this was  really they're very they're very  passionate about their election they are  but a lot of people didn't come out this  time in part because the weather was  lousy there was snow in the north and  and bad weather in the south so there  was some slight reduction in turnout  because of that but there were a lot of  people who are angry frustrated they  don't trust the government they don't  trust the established parties and I  think this movement was playing on all  of that frustration and anger and that's  really what brought people to this new  voice in the political system now let me  ask you this with all of the swirling  problems with the euro how is this  situation  is it going to create more anxiety in  terms of the debt crisis in Europe well  it creates a great deal of anxiety  because as you said at the outset after  last summer when Mario Draghi said the  European Central Bank will do whatever  it takes to keep the eurozone going  there's been a collective sigh of relief  and the eurozone seems to have  stabilized you know it's not the crisis  isn't over  but right now Europe is dealing with  Cyprus which is really a small issue  compared with Greece and Spain and the  issues it was facing a year ago but this  creates a huge problem potentially the  the prospect of instability in your--in  in Italy prospect of easing of austerity  which raises the prospect that perhaps  bond prices of the Europe of the Italian  debt will drop yields will increase and  perhaps we'll get to a situation where  Italy might need support down the road  now the real problem is that Italy's  debt is public debt is two trillion  euros and that's not billion that's  trillion two trillion euros it's more  public debt than Greece Portugal Ireland  and Spain put together and Italy is to  use the old cliche it's too big to fail  and it's also too big to bail and the  problem is that what Draghi said last  summer is predicated on a government  agreeing to conditions for fiscal reform  structural reform austerity policy a  whole range of reforms to improve their  financial and fiscal situation if that  happens the European Central Bank will  buy their bonds but the question is what  happens if you have a government that  doesn't want to negotiate the  conditions and accept the conditions  well then the ECB won't come in and buy  the bonds now we haven't had that kind  of situation yet but the prospect of a  government that would be easing on  austerity meaning the markets might get  a little more concerned about whether  Italy can pay back its debt of two  trillion euros over the over the near  term and longer term raises a prospect  of anxiety and a renewal of this of this  crisis that has now gone on for three  years  so the first reactions to the election  the election took place on this previous  Sunday and Monday the first reactions on  Tuesday were a sharp drop in in stock  markets and increase in yields and bond  yields in Europe it settled down a bit  now and and I think more observers see  the prospect of some kind of patchwork  government for at least the short term  but there is a lot of anxiety now that  this is now reopening a crisis that most  people had thought was settled so do you  think it will get worked out I thi